---
import SiteShell from "../layouts/SiteShell.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/common/PageHero.astro";
import Section from "../components/common/Section.astro";
import CtaBand from "../components/common/CtaBand.astro";
---

<SiteShell
  title="Manifesto — GazeKeeper"
  description="Software should protect attention, not just consume it. The principles GazeKeeper is built on."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Attention is the asset. Act accordingly."
    subtext="GazeKeeper exists because software spends your attention all day and almost nothing helps you keep it."
  />

  <Section>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every app on your Mac competes for the same finite resource. Notifications, badges, sync popups, “quick
        questions.” The industry built an economy on interrupting you, then shipped wellness features as an apology.
      </p>
      <p>
        A utility earns its place in your menu bar by being boring in the best sense. It does one job, at the right
        moments, and otherwise disappears. That belief produces four commitments.
      </p>

      <h2>Interruption is a cost, never a feature</h2>
      <p>
        A break reminder has to interrupt you, so it should interrupt as little as it can. GazeKeeper gives 60 seconds’
        notice, then waits for a pause in your typing. It holds the break while a camera or microphone is in use, while
        you share your screen, and while an app is fullscreen. You can snooze or skip.
      </p>

      <h2>Free, with nothing held hostage</h2>
      <p>
        GazeKeeper is free. There is no trial clock, no paid tier, and no account standing between you and the download.
      </p>

      <h2>Your data stays on your Mac</h2>
      <p>
        Settings and statistics live on your Mac. GazeKeeper checks whether the camera, microphone, or a screen share is
        in use, which app is in front, and how long since your last keypress. It never reads what you type or say. The
        only network request the app makes is the update check.
      </p>

      <h2>Health claims deserve humility</h2>
      <p>
        We cite research where it exists and say where it disagrees. The evidence for the <a href="/20-20-20-rule"
          >20-20-20 rule</a
        > is mixed: one trial found break reminders eased symptoms, and another found scheduled breaks did not. A break app
        cannot promise to protect your vision. It can get you to look away on a schedule, and that is the only promise this
        one makes.
      </p>

      <h2>Twenty seconds is the ask</h2>
      <p>Look at something far away. Let your shoulders drop. Blink. The screen will still be there when you get back.</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <CtaBand title="Try it for a day." subtext="Free, with no account." />
</SiteShell>
